      {
        "id": "def-small-set-minorization-and-drift",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Small-set minorization and geometric drift",
        "deps": [
          "def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel",
          "def-iterated-transition-kernels",
          "def-invariant-and-stationary-distribution-for-a-markov-kernel",
          "def-probability-measure"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "statement": "For a probability kernel $P$ on $(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal X)$ ([[def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel]]), a measurable set $C$ is **$(m,\\epsilon,\\nu)$-small** if $m\\ge1$ is an integer, $0<\\epsilon\\le1$, $\\nu$ is a probability measure ([[def-probability-measure]]), and\n$$P^m(x,A)\\ge\\epsilon\\nu(A)\\quad(x\\in C,\\ A\\in\\mathcal X).$$\nThe inequality is pointwise in $x$ and holds for every measurable $A$.\n\nA **geometric drift inequality outside $C$** consists of a finite-valued measurable $V:\\mathsf X\\to[1,\\infty)$, constants $0<\\lambda<1$ and $0\\le b<\\infty$, and\n$$PV(x):=\\int V(y)P(x,dy)\\le\\lambda V(x)+b\\,1_C(x)\\quad(x\\in\\mathsf X).$$\nThe terms small set and drift are separate hypotheses; neither definition asserts the existence of the other.\n\nWhen an invariant probability $\\pi$ is specified ([[def-invariant-and-stationary-distribution-for-a-markov-kernel]]), an **extended geometric drift certificate finite $\\pi$-almost everywhere** allows measurable $V:\\mathsf X\\to[1,+\\infty]$ with $\\pi(V<\\infty)=1$ and the same pointwise inequality, interpreted in the nonnegative extended reals. This is a separate convention from the finite-valued drift function above. The geometric theorem treats this certificate on $E=\\{V<\\infty\\}$; the original finite-valued convention gives the corresponding bound on the whole state space."
      },
