      {
        "id": "lem-bivariate-drift-minorization-coupling-rate",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "A bivariate drift and minorization bound geometric coupling error",
        "deps": [
          "cor-canonical-markov-chain-on-path-space",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel",
          "def-small-set-minorization-and-drift",
          "def-total-variation-distance-for-probability-laws",
          "lem-total-variation-coupling-and-kernel-contraction",
          "lem-finite-products-of-probability-kernels",
          "thm-discrete-strong-markov-property",
          "thm-measurability-of-integration-against-a-kernel"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "statement": "Let $Q$ be a probability kernel, $D$ be $(1,\\epsilon,\\nu)$-small for $Q$, and let $0<a<1$. If $0<\\epsilon<1$, define residual probabilities $R_x=(Q(x,\\cdot)-\\epsilon\\nu)/(1-\\epsilon)$ on $D$ and assume\n$$B\\ge\\max\\{1,(1-\\epsilon)\\sup_{(x,y)\\in D^2}R_x\\otimes R_y(h)/a\\}<\\infty.$$\nIf $\\epsilon=1$, set $B=1$ and impose no residual condition. Suppose $h:\\mathsf X^2\\to[1,\\infty)$ is finite-valued and measurable and $Q\\otimes Q\\,h\\le ah$ off $D^2$. Then there are $M<\\infty$ and $0<r<1$ such that a coupling of two $Q$-chains has a measurable split-success time $T$, with $X_n=Y_n$ on $\\{T\\le n\\}$, and satisfies\n$$\\Pr_{x,y}(T>n)\\le M h(x,y)r^n.$$\nConsequently $\\|Q^n(x,\\cdot)-Q^n(y,\\cdot)\\|_{\\rm TV}\\le M h(x,y)r^n$. For $\\epsilon=1$, the transition following the first visit to $D^2$ couples the chains."
      },
